---
title: Setting up specimen records
sidebar:
  label: Setup
  order: 1
---

The NAHPU default lists for `Specimen parts` and `Treatments` are intentionally
short. Before entering a large batch of specimens, configure the project lists
that your team will use in the field or collection room.

{% steps %}

1. Navigate to the dashboard.
2. Tap/click the hamburger menu in the top left corner.
3. Select `Settings`.
4. Select `Specimens`.
5. Configure the `Use project ID` toggle. It is off by default, which uses a
   cataloger's initials and field number. At least one Cataloger must have
   personal field-number registration enabled and a current number.

    Turn on `Use project ID` to configure a prefix, the next catalog number,
    and a suffix. NAHPU concatenates those values directly. Turn on
    auto-increment to assign and advance the project number when a new specimen
    uses this mode.

    This choice is only the default. Open a specimen's field-ID editor to choose
    Personnel or Project for that specimen. A project can contain both types,
    but one specimen cannot have both identifiers.

    {% aside type="caution" %}

    Changing a prefix or suffix changes how existing project IDs display.
    Those identifiers may already have been used in exports or written on
    specimen labels. Double-check the values before confirming the change.

    {% /aside %}

6. Add `Tissue ID` (optional).

    Some museums assign separate tissue numbers for the same voucher specimen.
    You can set up a prefix, the next number, and a suffix here. Leading zeros
    in the number are kept, so `M0042-T` is followed by `M0043-T`. NAHPU can
    then generate the next tissue number when you add a specimen part.

    For the Ichthyology catalog format, `Individual tag ID` sets the same kind
    of numbering for individuals in a fish lot.

7. Add `Specimen types`.

    Add all preparation and tissue types you expect to use, including types you
    may only collect occasionally.

8. Add `Treatments`.

    Treatments are used on specimen parts. Examples include `None`, `ETOH`,
    `Formalin`, `LN2`, and `DMSO`.

{% /steps %}

{% aside type="tip" %}

Specimen types and treatments are user configs. They can be exported and
imported with project archives so another NAHPU installation can use the same
controlled lists and export behavior.

See [Settings](/en/usages/settings/) for the complete configuration map.

{% /aside %}
